  "title": "CHAPTER 5 : GRAVITATION",
  "order": 98,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "ydwdd3p",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO GRAVITATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Universal Law of Gravitation details the mutual attraction between two masses in the universe.",
        "Acceleration due to gravity (g) is subject to variations based on location, depth, and altitude.",
        "Mass represents the quantity of matter in an object, while Weight measures the gravitational force acting upon it.",
        "Thrust, pressure, buoyancy, and Archimedes' principle govern the behavior of objects immersed in fluids."
      ],
      "id": "naf8bou"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Chapter",
          "value": "Chapter 5",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Subject Topic",
          "value": "Gravitation",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Constant",
          "value": "Gravitational Constant (G)",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Core Law",
          "value": "Archimedes' Principle",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "nqzoz3s"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9mixnva",
      "content": "Gravitation is the force by which every object in the universe attracts every other object. This force is responsible for:"
    },
    {
      "id": "nth10gd",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Falling of objects on Earth</li><li>Motion of planets around the Sun</li><li>Motion of moon around Earth</li><li>Formation of tides</li></ul>",
        "Falling of objects on Earth",
        "Motion of planets around the Sun",
        "Motion of moon around Earth",
        "Formation of tides"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ytd0alq",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "f02jkw2",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GENERAL KNOWLEDGE/images/image_f02jkw2.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jiub1ma",
      "content": "The force of gravitation is a universal force, which means it acts between all objects having mass."
    },
    {
      "id": "idn09g4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "UNIVERSAL LAW OF GRAVITATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qk1dlxw",
      "content": "The Universal Law of Gravitation was given by Sir Isaac Newton. According to this law:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gsiti6n",
      "content": "Every object in the universe attracts every other object with a force which is:"
    },
    {
      "id": "mfcy08m",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Directly proportional to the product of their masses</li><li>Inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres</li></ul>",
        "Directly proportional to the product of their masses",
        "Inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "e0whg2p",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "wjj6bhw",
      "src": "/books/Guide/GENERAL KNOWLEDGE/images/image_wjj6bhw.png"
    },
    {
      "id": "f78mthm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Mathematical Formula"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4gdht53",
      "content": "F = G × (m₁m₂ / r²) Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2ecqi3r",
      "content": "F = gravitational force m₁ = mass of first object"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nmyo091",
      "content": "m₂ = mass of second object"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "14bran9",
      "content": "r = distance between the centres G = gravitational constant"
    },
    {
      "id": "h7qrv3i",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Value of Gravitational Constant"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ke24dos",
      "content": "G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² / kg²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c4ik0io",
      "content": "This value is constant everywhere in the universe."
    },
    {
      "id": "w7dqxgg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "EFFECTS OF GRAVITATIONAL FORCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ggxq2f9",
      "content": "Gravitational force is responsible for:"
    },
    {
      "id": "09xh97l",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Objects falling towards Earth</li><li>Planets revolving around the Sun</li><li>Formation of tides</li><li>Weight of bodies</li><li>Holding atmosphere to Earth</li></ul>",
        "Objects falling towards Earth",
        "Planets revolving around the Sun",
        "Formation of tides",
        "Weight of bodies",
        "Holding atmosphere to Earth"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "8m7kzjn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "FREE FALL"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hykzzc4",
      "content": "When an object falls towards the Earth under the influence of gravitational force only, it is said to be in free fall."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fvhwev3",
      "content": "In free fall:"
    },
    {
      "id": "f7wacqv",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Only gravitational force acts</li><li>Air resistance is neglected</li></ul>",
        "Only gravitational force acts",
        "Air resistance is neglected"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "oxn6uih",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Acceleration Due to Gravity (g)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p015ac1",
      "content": "The acceleration produced in a body due to gravitational pull of Earth is called acceleration due to gravity."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "w9m9r0r",
      "content": "Symbol: g"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fs4m2sj",
      "content": "Value on Earth = 9.8 m/s² It is constant for all objects."
    },
    {
      "id": "96dcoex",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "MASS AND WEIGHT"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "CHAPTER 5 : GRAVITATION",
      "id": "jbnwrwq"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "92lihhv",
      "content": "Mass:"
    },
    {
      "id": "ifqs45r",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Amount of matter in a body</li><li>Constant everywhere</li><li>SI unit = kilogram</li><li>Scalar quantity</li></ul>",
        "Amount of matter in a body",
        "Constant everywhere",
        "SI unit = kilogram",
        "Scalar quantity"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dg0kf67",
      "content": "Weight:"
    },
    {
      "id": "j8pr874",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Force with which Earth attracts a body</li><li>Changes with place</li><li>SI unit = Newton</li><li>Vector quantity</li></ul>",
        "Force with which Earth attracts a body",
        "Changes with place",
        "SI unit = Newton",
        "Vector quantity"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "73obb2j",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Formula of Weight"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r433gv2",
      "content": "Weight = Mass × Acceleration due to gravity W = mg"
    },
    {
      "id": "jtfe4wm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Difference Between Mass and Weight"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nf0nnaj",
      "content": "Mass:"
    },
    {
      "id": "l5kdqzq",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Constant</li><li>Scalar</li><li>kg</li><li>Does not change</li></ul>",
        "Constant",
        "Scalar",
        "kg",
        "Does not change"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e4bdop7",
      "content": "Weight:"
    },
    {
      "id": "vdayddb",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Changes with place</li><li>Vector</li><li>Newton</li><li>Depends on gravity</li></ul>",
        "Changes with place",
        "Vector",
        "Newton",
        "Depends on gravity"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "lajnpsp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "VARIATION OF GRAVITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vvq4e9y",
      "content": "The value of g changes with:"
    },
    {
      "id": "xt50fj3",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Height</li><li>Depth</li><li>Latitude</li></ul>",
        "Height",
        "Depth",
        "Latitude"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Property",
        "Mass",
        "Weight"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Definition",
          "Quantity of matter contained in a body",
          "Force with which a body is attracted towards Earth"
        ],
        [
          "Quantity Type",
          "Scalar quantity",
          "Vector quantity"
        ],
        [
          "Constancy",
          "Remains constant everywhere",
          "Varies from place to place depending on 'g'"
        ],
        [
          "SI Unit",
          "Kilogram (kg)",
          "Newton (N)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "035bfos"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ggp68vd",
      "content": "g decreases with height. g decreases with depth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lp07n56",
      "content": "g is maximum at poles and minimum at equator."
    },
    {
      "id": "tmpda41",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "THRUST AND PRESSURE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "17kwr4q",
      "content": "Thrust:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k3xq2ci",
      "content": "Force acting perpendicular to a surface. Pressure:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jadw7rh",
      "content": "Force acting per unit area."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0dvwpa5",
      "content": "Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x6zm8b6",
      "content": "Pressure = Force / Area SI unit = Pascal (Pa)"
    },
    {
      "id": "csakyrh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "BUOYANCY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jssicqc",
      "content": "The upward force exerted by a fluid on an object immersed in it is called buoyant force. Due to buoyancy:"
    },
    {
      "id": "909dp4v",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Objects float</li><li>Objects sink</li></ul>",
        "Objects float",
        "Objects sink"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "9k0tzaf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ARCHIMEDES' PRINCIPLE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rn5x0kh",
      "content": "According to Archimedes' Principle:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k5azele",
      "content": "When a body is immersed in a fluid, it experiences an upward force equal to the weight of the fluid displaced."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "is992ch",
      "content": "This principle is used in:"
    },
    {
      "id": "zphw1gj",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Ships</li><li>Submarines</li><li>Hydrometers</li></ul>",
        "Ships",
        "Submarines",
        "Hydrometers"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "46lieu8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "FLOATATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "og5tm8w",
      "content": "An object floats if:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uee0kxj",
      "content": "Density of object &lt; Density of liquid"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Frequently tested: Distinguish between Mass (scalar, constant) and Weight (vector, variable).",
        "Pay attention to the mathematical formulation of Universal Law of Gravitation and value/units of Gravitational Constant (G).",
        "Conceptual problems on Buoyancy, Thrust, Pressure, and Archimedes' Principle are recurring exam favorites."
      ],
      "id": "law70jh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zk8p9ti",
      "content": "An object sinks if:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2n0iize",
      "content": "Density of object &gt; Density of liquid"
    },
    {
      "id": "0faxcrv",
